From dafdd6f96e984e3da131bd908462c956ce0806f7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Erik Bitzek <e.bitzek@mpie.de>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 18:36:01 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 11/12] Phase A (natoms bugfix session 2026-08-29): per-trial
 parallel-invariant diagnostics

Instrument fix mc/sites so a parallel count corruption is caught at the
trial where it happens, with the identity of the site/tag, instead of
surfacing as the vague block-end 'sum of nlocal != natoms' abort:

- unconditional owner-count assertion on every insertion/deletion/source
  trial (one int allreduce; a trial already pays a full energy
  evaluation): exactly one rank must execute the trial. Catches
  no-owner insertions (H1), double-owner deletions (H2), no-owner
  deletions (H4) with site coords, catalogue index, tag, box bounds and
  periodicity in the message.
- check yes: per-trial rank-uniformity assertion over {accept decision,
  natoms, mu, beta, energy_after, energy_before} (MIN/MAX allreduce);
  any cross-rank divergence of the Metropolis inputs (H3 family) is
  named at its birth, before the accept/reject branches can cascade.
- check yes: MC-block-start checks: natoms vs sum(nlocal) BEFORE the
  block (attributes corruption to the MD segment / other fixes, not the
  MC), and sum(nlocal) across the block-start exchange (attributes
  genuine atom loss through a non-periodic boundary, H5).
- check #1 failure forensics: occupied catalogue entries whose tag no
  rank (or 2+ ranks) resolves, plus a cross-rank duplicate-tag census.
- test-only hook MCS_DEBUG_PERTURB_ENERGY=<rank>: alternating-sign
  energy_full() offset on one rank, to exercise the divergence
  diagnostics deterministically (inert unless the env var is set).

Gate A: 44/44 suite green on build/lmp; file-mode no-owner site and
np=2 perturbed-energy injections both produce the new precise errors.
Injection also reproduces the campaign signature: with checks off, a
single divergent trial cascades to 'Lost atoms: original 12 current 11'
(natoms high by 1, the observed sign) and, seed-dependent, to a
2-ranks-own-the-trial deletion -- the 'deletion path' as a downstream
symptom of a rank-divergent trial.
